9592: 0000 0011 1111        64 MB
9593: 0000 0111 1111       128 MB
9594: 0000 1111 1111       256 MB
9595: 0001 1111 1111       512 MB
9596: 0011 1111 1111          1 GB
9597: 0111 1111 1111          2 GB
9598: 1111 1111 1111          4 GB
9599: 
9600: IOC_W_BASE register bits <31:20> specify the target window starting address
9601: in the PCI memory address space. The IOC_W_BASE and IOC_W_MASK
9602: registers determine when a PCI transaction references an address in a target
9603: window, as follows:
9604: 
9605: 1. Each of the incoming PCI address bits <31:20> is exclusive-ORed with
9606:      the corresponding bit of each window base register (IOC_W_BASE1,
9607:      IOC_W_BASE0, bits <31:20>).
9608: 
9609: 2. The result of each exclusive-OR is then ANDed with the complement
9610:      of the corresponding bits of the corresponding window mask register
9611:      (IOC_W_MASK1, IOC_W_MASK0, bits <31:20>).
9612: 
9613: 3. The result of the AND operation for each window is then ORed to produce
9614:      a hit (match) or miss indication. If the result of the final OR is a zero for
9615:      a given window, the incoming address has hit in that PCI target window;
9616:      otherwise, it has missed in that window.
9617: 
9618:                                                     Note
9619: 
9620:      The window base address must be on a naturally aligned address
9621:      boundary equal to the window size (Table 6-6). Additionally, to ensure
9622:      that only one PCI target window can match an incoming PCI address,
9623:      the PCI target windows must be programmed so that they do not
9624:      respond to overlapping PCI address ranges.
9625: 
9626: 6-24 I/O Controller
9627:             Each target window is independently enabled when the window enable bit
9628:             (WEN) is set in the corresponding IOC_W_BASE register. The IOC responds
9629:             only to PCI-initiated transfers that hit in an enabled window. If the WEN bit
9630:             is cleared, the IOC ignores PCI-initiated transfers that hit in that window.
9631:             When a hit occurs in an enabled PCI target window, the IOC responds to
9632:             the PCI cycle by asserting the devsel_l signal. The IOC translates the
9633:             32-bit PCI address to a 34-bit CPU address. The scatter-gather (SG) bit
9634:             in the corresponding IOC_W_BASE register determines how the address
9635:             is translated. If the SG bit is clear, direct-mapped address translation is
9636:             used (Section 6.3.2.1). If the SG bit is set, scatter-gather mapped address
9637:             translation is used (Section 6.3.2.2).
9638: 6.3.2.1 Direct-Mapped Address Translation
9639:             Direct-mapped address translation is used when an incoming PCI address
9640:             hits in a PCI target window and the SG bit is not set in the corresponding
10130: 
10131:             � If the lock_l signal is sampled asserted, the transfer is a nonexclusive
10132:                   access. If this nonexclusive cycle addresses a location in an enabled PCI
10133:                   target window, the IOC uses the PCI target-disconnect protocol to signal a
10134:                   retry, and no data is transferred.
10135: 
10136:             � If the lock_l signal is sampled deasserted, the transfer is a continuation of
10137:                   the exclusive access. If this exclusive access cycle addresses a location in
10138:                   an enabled PCI target window, the IOC completes the access normally (the
10139:                   IOC does not retry the access).
10140: 
10141: 6.3.11 Guaranteed Access Arbitration
10142: 
10143:             In some applications, to minimize access latency, a PCI device may need to win
10144:             arbitration for memory before initiating a PCI cycle. The IOC supports two
10145:             PCI sideband signals for memory arbitration, memreq_l and memack_l. Both
10146:             signals are synchronous to the PCI clock signal pci_clk_in.
10147: 
10148:             A device wins arbitration for memory according to the following sequence:
10149: 
10150:             1. The device must assert the memreq_l signal to the IOC.
10151: 
10152:             2. When the IOC samples the memreq_l signal asserted, it arbitrates
10153:                   (internal to the chip) for access to memory.
10154: 
10155: 6-36 I/O Controller
10156:             3. When the IOC wins arbitration for memory, it asserts the memack_l
10157:                   signal. While memack_l is asserted, the CPU is blocked from accessing
10158:                   memory.
10159: 
10160:             4. When the requesting device samples the memack_l signal asserted (and if
10161:                   it has won arbitration for the PCI bus), it can initiate a PCI cycle without
10162:                   any intervening CPU access or additional latency.
10163: 
10164:             The memack_l signal remains asserted for one or more cycles after the device
10165:             deasserts the memreq_l signal. The number of cycles depends on the ratio of
10166:             the internal clock frequency to the PCI clock frequency.
10167:             To avoid buffering a large number of write operations, the IOC allows only
10168:             one longword per DMA-write transaction while memack_l is asserted. After
10169:             the first longword, the IOC terminates the transaction by using the target
10170:             disconnect protocol.
10171:             When the internal clock is 166 MHz and the PCI clock is 33 MHz, and if
10172:             memack_l is asserted before frame_l is asserted, the delay from the assertion
10173:             of frame_l to the assertion of trdy_l is less than 2 �s.
10174: 
10175: 6.4 I/O Controller Registers
10176: 
10177:             The IOC implements a number of registers to control its operation and
10178:             facilitate its testing. The CPU can access the IOC registers directly, but they
10179:             are not accessible from the PCI bus.
10180:             All references to the IOC registers must allow for quadword access; longword
10181:             access is not supported.
10182:             The IOC registers are described in ascending-address order in Sections 6.4.1
10183:             through 6.4.14. Unless stated otherwise, the assertion of reset_in_l does not
10184:             modify any IOC register bits and all IOC register bits are UNDEFINED after
10185:             a power-up reset.
10186: 
10187:                                                                  Note
10188:                   In the register field description tables, the abbreviations in the Type
10189:                   column indicate field access behavior. The abbreviations are defined in
10190:                   the Conventions section of the Preface.
10191: 
10192:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-37
10193: 6.4.1 Host Address Extension Register
10194: 
10195:             In the sparse memory space, the lower bits of the CPU address indicate
10196:             transfer size, and the address is shifted down by 5 bits during the translation
10197:             from CPU address to PCI address. The host address extension (IOC_HAE)
10198:             register extends the shifted address to 32 bits.
10199: 
10200:             The IOC_HAE register is accessed at address 18000000016. All bits of this
10201:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
